WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.311
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.311 --> 00:00:07.691
Jamie, welcome and thank you very much for
coming here today.

00:00:07.691 --> 00:00:09.850
&gt;&gt; Pleasure to be here,
thank you, everybody.

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:14.570
I noticed a lot of other folks here I
didn't expect to be here, so welcome,

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:15.990
friends, from other places.

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:19.539
&gt;&gt; This is the third you've
come to talk to us and

00:00:19.539 --> 00:00:23.234
I have to ask are we just very lucky to
have you or is there some perhaps latent

00:00:23.234 --> 00:00:27.307
regret in choosing our competitor on the
east coast for your own business school?

00:00:27.307 --> 00:00:27.949
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So

00:00:27.949 --> 00:00:32.290
why, my wife went to Harvard Business
School, I went to Harvard Business School,

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:34.352
my daughter went to
Harvard Business School and

00:00:34.352 --> 00:00:38.190
two son-in-laws went to
Harvard Business School.

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:38.960
But we love Stanford.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:41.530
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I like to come out here all the time.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:45.786
When I come out here I realize why
you don't work so hard out here.

00:00:45.786 --> 00:00:47.725
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:47.725 --> 00:00:49.195
&gt;&gt; Now speaking of business school,

00:00:49.195 --> 00:00:52.725
we have many people in the room who are
trying to decide between entrepreneurship

00:00:52.725 --> 00:00:54.965
and more traditional paths on leaving.

00:00:54.965 --> 00:00:58.495
Do you mind talking to the decision you
made on leaving Harvard yourself, and

00:00:58.495 --> 00:01:01.465
if you'd do the same thing today?

00:01:01.465 --> 00:01:06.170
&gt;&gt; So when I'd worked at a small
really second rate consulting company

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:08.700
before business school,
back then they had a deferred admit, so

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.570
I was admitted but
I had to work for two years.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:13.120
And I did the consulting thing,

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.730
you learn a lot, I also learned I
didn't want to be a consultant.

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:20.240
But I saw a whole bunch of different
companies and industries, including badly

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:26.270
run political, all these various things,
so it was actually a very good experience.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:29.930
But when I got to Harvard Business School,
I actually worked at Goldman-Sachs

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:35.670
the summer of 81 and I was headed
back to Goldman, Morgan, or Lehman.

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:38.400
I was probably going to decide Goldman,
and at the last minute,

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.520
Sandy Weill offered me
a job to be his assistant.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:42.300
He had just sold Shearson,

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:46.130
which is a brokerage firm with a couple
thousand brokers through American Express.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:49.860
And it just looked like it was just too
good to not to see a large corporation

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:51.410
from the inside, how it goes.

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:53.950
My goal in life was to be
part of an organization.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:57.590
It wasn't necessarily to be an investment
banker or a trader or something like that.

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:00.592
So I took the crap shoot,
I figured, worse comes to worst,

00:02:00.592 --> 00:02:01.850
I can go back to Wall Street.

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:04.630
So I didn't feel like I was
going to lose anything but time.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.990
Yes, I would make the same decision again.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:10.836
Maybe not for the same guy again but
the same decision.

00:02:10.836 --> 00:02:15.250
&gt;&gt; Now, Samuel Weill obviously
saw something in you at the time.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:17.930
When you meet men and
women our age at JP Morgan today,

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:20.465
what leadership characteristics
are you looking for in them

00:02:20.465 --> 00:02:24.560
,and how do you identify and bring those
people through as the next generation?

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.105
&gt;&gt; When you're young, it's hard to
say leadership characteristics but

00:02:27.105 --> 00:02:29.320
there's just some basic stuff.

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:32.420
There are people who know a lot.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.480
There's some people walk
into your office and

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:38.230
say, I'd like to know about the strategy
of the company and they didn't bother to

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:40.860
read the chairman's letter I wrote
which is 30 or 40 pages long.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:44.390
There are people who walk in and say,
you really should be thinking about doing

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:47.610
better digital services but
they don't look at the ones we do do.

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.830
The other people walk in and
they know everything, and so

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:51.940
when they have a conversation,

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:56.220
they're actually enhancing your life
as opposed to the other way around.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:59.980
So character is a sine qua non and
by character,

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:02.490
I also mean that they tell the whole
truth, nothing but the truth.

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:05.240
They don't shave the truth and they say
the same thing to you that they say to

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:08.530
you, because the second I see people
doing something different than that,

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:11.750
you just go on a list to me,
I have no interest.

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:15.320
I don't have time for that and
I won't have interest in it, and

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:19.200
people who are good relate well to
other people, so a variety of things.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.840
&gt;&gt; I think when we look at finances,
often people

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.130
concentrate on the competitive leadership
characteristics they see there.

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:29.350
At the GSB,
we have a real folks on the collaborative.

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:31.801
How do you see the balance between
competitive and collaborative [CROSSTALK].

00:03:31.801 --> 00:03:33.930
&gt;&gt; Competitive inside the company?

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:34.930
&gt;&gt; Inside and out.

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:38.811
&gt;&gt; I think the things that destroy
companies, folks, are bureaucracy and

00:03:38.811 --> 00:03:39.460
politics.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:45.660
We had an offsite this summer, I asking
many times, we gotta make this simple,

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:49.190
we gotta make this better,
we gotta kill our meetings are too long,

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:52.850
and we stand behind regulations sometimes,
the regulators are making us do it.

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:55.700
But I said no, we're going to re-imagine
all these things the way they should be,

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:56.980
not the way they are today and

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:01.360
let's take a crack at killing some of this
bureaucracy that's grown up recently.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:04.580
And someone wrote a memo,
and we all do pre-reads.

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:07.860
In my meetings, you don't flip through
a chart, it's all pre-reads, I expect you

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:11.860
to do your homework, get the analysis and
part it up with people beforehand.

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:13.980
So you don't walk in and say,
I want to do a, b, and c but

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:15.290
I haven't spoken to this person yet.

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:17.420
I would say,
why didn't you speak to this person?

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:18.780
The meeting is cancelled.

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:22.200
Go do that first and then come back with a
joint recommendation or that you disagree

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:25.820
and then we'll talk about specifically
why you disagree about something.

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:27.642
Anyway, I'd asked about bureaucracy and

00:04:27.642 --> 00:04:30.426
the pre-read by saying bureaucracy
is a initially a condition at

00:04:30.426 --> 00:04:33.790
the output of complex businesses
operating in complex global environments.

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:36.992
It's just not true, bureaucracy kills.

00:04:36.992 --> 00:04:40.130
Bureaucracy drives out good people,
it drives out innovation.

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:43.150
It makes the person in the office
next to you a competitor and

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:46.810
not a collaborator and
that's a really bad idea inside a company.

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:48.400
Now it's okay to compete with ideas but

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:51.390
it's really bad idea when you're
competing with the person next to you.

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:55.100
It creates politics and if you look at
what's killed companies over the years,

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:56.260
it was bureaucracy.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:58.700
When you look at the Fortune 500 and
you did a 1950, 1960,

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:01.290
1970, 1980,
look at the companies who failed,

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:06.480
it was because they were dumb,
bureaucratic, backward and political.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:11.120
CEOs should never allow that stuff and
yet they do, as a normal course,

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:15.440
they allow it and you've seen a lot of
public companies and boards allow it.

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:16.680
Boards can create it, by the way.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:19.053
So absolute collaboration, but

00:05:19.053 --> 00:05:23.430
collaboration does not mean
everyone has to agree.

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:26.237
Collaboration does not mean
you go a long to get along,

00:05:26.237 --> 00:05:28.628
because that's very often
what people mean by it.

00:05:28.628 --> 00:05:32.404
He's a good guy, if you walk into me and
say I'm going to be loyal to you,

00:05:32.404 --> 00:05:37.430
l would question what that means because
you should be loyal to the institution.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:41.033
You should be loyal to the customers,
you could be loyal to the principles for

00:05:41.033 --> 00:05:44.469
which l stand, but being loyal to me
usually means you're going to defend my

00:05:44.469 --> 00:05:47.646
decision whether or not you agree,
which I just think is a mistake.

00:05:47.646 --> 00:05:50.197
&gt;&gt; You're well known for
having a very candid and open,

00:05:50.197 --> 00:05:52.460
forthright style of communication.

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:55.600
It's appreciated by Warren Buffett, he
says he loves your chairman letters, and

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:56.530
we obviously appreciate it here.

00:05:56.530 --> 00:06:00.040
What was it that led you to develop
this style of communication early on?

00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:04.675
&gt;&gt; I'm one of those who I don't think
I've changed much since I've been eight.

00:06:04.675 --> 00:06:08.420
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; When I got to Harvard Business School,

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:11.680
some of the advice I got when I was
graduating was whatever you do,

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:15.843
Jamie, don't go into corporate America,
you won't survive.

00:06:15.843 --> 00:06:18.600
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And there was truth to that,

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:21.930
I was a little too outspoken for
some corporations etc,

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:24.640
and part of it was Warren Buffet.

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:30.020
When I was in high school or college,
I actually read his partnership letters.

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:34.210
He would say, I'll speak to you as if
you're my smart sister who doesn't know

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:37.350
everything I know, so I have to go
out of my way to explain it to you.

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:38.870
Business is not that complicated,

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:41.470
we should be able to explain it,
I always felt exactly the same way.

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:45.670
I always felt as a matter of
principle inside a company,

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:50.880
if I allow spinning to the public,
you are going to spin to me.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:55.000
Think of earnings, numbers, how great
we are doing when we're not and so

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:59.090
I say the same thing to you that I say to
my board, that I say to my shareholder,

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:01.740
I say to my employee,
that I say to my customer.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:02.720
There is no difference.

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:04.180
I might phrase it differently
because you're talking to

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:06.810
different group of people and
you're trying to explain yourself, but

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:09.410
there is no difference,
and that clarity, I think,

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:12.670
helps a company know what it's
trying to accomplish all the time.

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:16.780
When I read annual letters, and it's all
gobbledygook in the letter, my respect for

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:19.440
the company drops a little bit.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:22.340
And then you gotta be very specific,
because we can all say,

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:24.935
the customer is the most important thing,
and the customer, the customer.

00:07:24.935 --> 00:07:28.140
But it should mean
something specific like,

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:31.220
do you actually read
customer complaints and

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:35.550
then you do something about it as
opposed to, we put the customer first.

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:38.910
A lot of people say that and they don't
mean it in any way, shape, or form.

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:41.640
They do no actions that would
actually support the statement.

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:46.030
&gt;&gt; So this one I'm afraid I'm going to
try to sneak in some more career advice.

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:49.240
I think everyone here is aware that
tech is extremely sexy right now.

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:51.480
&gt;&gt; A little bit less so today.

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:52.665
&gt;&gt; Don't say today.

00:07:52.665 --> 00:07:56.420
[LAUGH] What do you think is the
opportunity in a career in finance that

00:07:56.420 --> 00:07:58.920
some in audience maybe aren't seeing?

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:00.030
&gt;&gt; First of all, one thing about tech,

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:02.850
tech is driven I don't think it's
different than it was before.

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:06.370
It's driven change throughout
all of history, and

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:10.550
all various forms of technology
from the actual chips to Internet.

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:14.810
But also printing, energy, institutions,

00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:19.600
schools, the churches,
the dissemination of morality.

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:22.085
That's what's always changed society.

00:08:22.085 --> 00:08:25.525
It's always at the root,
it's at the root of changes in businesses,

00:08:25.525 --> 00:08:28.250
it's at the root of changes
in what companies do well and

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:31.028
what companies don't do well,
so it's absolutely critical.

00:08:31.028 --> 00:08:34.480
I personally think Silicon Valley
kind of got a free ride with Obama in

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:35.380
the White House.

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:38.414
You pandered to him and
he pandered to you,

00:08:38.414 --> 00:08:41.712
now you're seeing a little
flip side of that.

00:08:41.712 --> 00:08:44.294
So the big companies,
the successful ones are going to find out,

00:08:44.294 --> 00:08:45.568
which I've known a long time.

00:08:45.568 --> 00:08:49.236
The bigger you are, the more the
government's going to get inside what you

00:08:49.236 --> 00:08:52.121
do, and want to impose on you and
set rules and standards,

00:08:52.121 --> 00:08:54.673
and sometimes for good,
sometimes not for good.

00:08:54.673 --> 00:08:56.804
But, so financial services, so

00:08:56.804 --> 00:09:00.390
I think technology's
an absolutely wonderful thing.

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:03.998
But financial services, first of all,
we are a huge user of technology.

00:09:03.998 --> 00:09:08.200
We spend $9 billion a year, so
we're not at the bleeding edge, but

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:09.490
we're damn close.

00:09:09.490 --> 00:09:13.590
In terms of what we spend money on, what
we do, our data centers, our capabilities,

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:15.080
our mobile, our digital.

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:19.169
We move $6 trillion around
the world every day digitally,

00:09:19.169 --> 00:09:23.350
not cryptocurrency, but
close to it because it's digital.

00:09:23.350 --> 00:09:27.060
And we bank 50 million
people on our households and

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:30.810
soon we're going to have online investing
and robo investing and all these

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:35.780
various things to link in to our accounts,
so we are huge users of that stuff.

00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:38.622
We also use the advertising
on Facebook and Google, etc.

00:09:38.622 --> 00:09:42.729
But financial services is always
going to be an interesting business,

00:09:42.729 --> 00:09:45.844
because it's not the most
important business, but

00:09:45.844 --> 00:09:49.267
it is at the nexus of how
a capitalist society functions.

00:09:49.267 --> 00:09:50.510
It's part of the spinning wheel.

00:09:50.510 --> 00:09:54.650
And when I say financial service, I'm
talking about all of it, private equity,

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:58.730
venture capital, activist, whether you
like them or not, banks, nonbanks,

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:00.000
individual investors.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.039
A lot of companies that
do things individually,

00:10:02.039 --> 00:10:04.200
that help change the world a little bit.

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:07.555
But it's always going to this field
that when money gets invested,

00:10:07.555 --> 00:10:09.220
the ideas come together.

00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:12.510
Things get executed,
capital gets executed globally.

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:15.574
So it's always fascinating,
it'll always be well paid,

00:10:15.574 --> 00:10:17.960
whatever some of these folks say in DC.

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:22.300
It has to be well paid because you can't
do it with dumb people making dumb

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:25.580
decisions, if you think you can actually
run a good economy or something like that.

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:28.210
It will always be a high tech user, and

00:10:28.210 --> 00:10:31.400
it will always be complex and
global by its nature.

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:33.040
And so it's a fascinating business.

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:35.720
When you wake up in the morning,
when I read the paper,

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:38.640
there's very little in the paper that
I'm not reading and thinking about.

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:41.280
It affects our clients, it affects
our companies, it affects our risk.

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:42.830
It affects our exposure,

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:45.760
we should be doing something about it,
like just any article.

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:49.035
because we do business in well over
a hundred countries, and with just about

00:10:49.035 --> 00:10:52.672
every country and every sovereign wealth,
and so it's a fascinating field.

00:10:52.672 --> 00:10:55.290
I mean, when you get into it,
there are all different parts to it.

00:10:55.290 --> 00:10:59.120
And you gotta decide if you want to get
into it, how you want to get into that.

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:01.930
One of the things we set up,
which I really like, is,

00:11:01.930 --> 00:11:06.950
we hired top-notch people from investment
banking, okay, and sales and trading.

00:11:06.950 --> 00:11:10.050
So we go to the best schools in the world,
we hire some of the best people.

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:13.060
We now do the same thing for
general management, about eight or nine or

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:16.630
ten a year because we need people
who can run the damn joint, too.

00:11:16.630 --> 00:11:19.227
And I want the same quality and
we move around the company,

00:11:19.227 --> 00:11:20.447
kind of keep an eye on them.

00:11:20.447 --> 00:11:24.349
But the point is, they want to be global,
they want to move around, and

00:11:24.349 --> 00:11:27.194
they want to learn how to manage and
run something.

00:11:27.194 --> 00:11:30.590
So the same kind of compensation
as investment bankers,

00:11:30.590 --> 00:11:34.090
same openness to senior people,
so that we can get them.

00:11:34.090 --> 00:11:37.220
Not that if you're an investment banker,
you can't move into management or

00:11:37.220 --> 00:11:38.038
sales and trading,

00:11:38.038 --> 00:11:41.139
but this is really people want to do
more management of financial services.

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:44.970
&gt;&gt; You mentioned cryptocurrency.

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:47.020
I know you said you weren't going
to talk about Bitcoin anymore,

00:11:47.020 --> 00:11:48.060
but you're at Stanford.

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:49.458
Some of us put all our
student loan money into it.

00:11:49.458 --> 00:11:52.919
And I have to ask you anyway,
why do you believe that governments and

00:11:52.919 --> 00:11:56.382
regulators can't allow the existence
of a global currency based on

00:11:56.382 --> 00:11:58.911
[CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; I've become like a spokesman against

00:11:58.911 --> 00:12:01.007
BitCoin, I don't care about BitCoin.

00:12:01.007 --> 00:12:02.457
I could give a shit,
to tell you the truth.

00:12:02.457 --> 00:12:03.731
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

