are you going to change wages on
day 1 of the 12 Corbett Tax Reform?

00:24:20.530 --> 00:24:21.692
That's their measure.

00:24:21.692 --> 00:24:22.907
What happened last time?

00:24:22.907 --> 00:24:24.520
That's their measure.

00:24:24.520 --> 00:24:27.110
The measure you should have
is the counter factual.

00:24:27.110 --> 00:24:30.161
Had you had competitive
tax systems 10 years ago,

00:24:30.161 --> 00:24:33.777
there would probably have been
$3 trillion retained here.

00:24:33.777 --> 00:24:37.070
Companies and capital,
5,000 companies have been bought by

00:24:37.070 --> 00:24:41.420
foreign companies because it's advantages
for them to own an American company now.

00:24:41.420 --> 00:24:43.855
So while our government
dawdled on these inversions,

00:24:43.855 --> 00:24:46.370
5,000 companies were acquired
by foreign companies,

00:24:46.370 --> 00:24:49.500
net, there's a study that came out by
ENY that would have been retained here.

00:24:49.500 --> 00:24:54.730
Think of corporate headquarters and
we damaged ourselves and it's permanent.

00:24:54.730 --> 00:24:57.210
And that's 4 trillion overseas,
and it's 4 trillion,

00:24:57.210 --> 00:25:01.140
someone has been reinvesting hard
equipment in plant, and companies.

00:25:01.140 --> 00:25:02.632
But there is still 2.5 trillion in cash.

00:25:02.632 --> 00:25:04.081
That's why it is there.

00:25:04.081 --> 00:25:06.749
And it's not because these companies
are moral, it's because it's so

00:25:06.749 --> 00:25:08.880
much more advantageous
to do stuff over there.

00:25:08.880 --> 00:25:11.800
Microsoft built a research
center in Vancouver

00:25:11.800 --> 00:25:16.030
because they can hire the same kids in
Seattle because of our immigration laws.

00:25:16.030 --> 00:25:18.155
And so
these things have held back America.

00:25:18.155 --> 00:25:19.863
And I am absolutely convinced and

00:25:19.863 --> 00:25:22.615
it's hard to prove it because
you can't put a model.

00:25:22.615 --> 00:25:26.740
We're growing at 2% in spite of the dumb
things government's done for 10 years.

00:25:26.740 --> 00:25:29.640
And had we done the right things around
competitive taxes, infrastructure,

00:25:29.640 --> 00:25:33.010
education, we'd be growing at 3% or more.

00:25:33.010 --> 00:25:35.930
This recovery,
the growth is half of a normal recovery.

00:25:37.120 --> 00:25:38.460
A small business formation,

00:25:38.460 --> 00:25:43.050
we're down net millions of small companies
that normally would have been formed.

00:25:43.050 --> 00:25:46.950
And the reason is lack of
access to credit, regulations.

00:25:46.950 --> 00:25:48.716
And again I can't prove it, I'm trying,

00:25:48.716 --> 00:25:51.818
we're using big data now to try to get
some of the stuff looking backwards.

00:25:51.818 --> 00:25:56.249
And so part of the agenda of the Trump
administration is the right stuff,

00:25:56.249 --> 00:26:01.131
infrastructure, education,
competitive tax reform, proper regulation.

00:26:01.131 --> 00:26:04.142
All right, I'll just say proper
as opposed to less or more,

00:26:04.142 --> 00:26:06.150
they massively overdid it.

00:26:06.150 --> 00:26:09.602
You've heard people say that
didn't hold back landing, okay.

00:26:09.602 --> 00:26:14.460
And we did some real analysis and
done by economist, not by me.

00:26:14.460 --> 00:26:20.080
Mortgages alone, we believe held back
by $1-2 trillion because of the cost,

00:26:20.080 --> 00:26:24.520
lack of securitization,
lack of servicing requirements and

00:26:24.520 --> 00:26:28.750
because of litigation, stuff around it
that people are mostly mortgage under rise

00:26:28.750 --> 00:26:33.390
are willing to make mortgages to first
time buyers, young people, immigrants,

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:35.390
private defaults and self employed.

00:26:35.390 --> 00:26:37.980
Not going back to
the sub-prime of the old age,

00:26:37.980 --> 00:26:40.200
just open up the credit box a little bit,
okay?

00:26:40.200 --> 00:26:44.450
$2 trillion of mortgages would've
been probably 5% growth in GDP.

00:26:44.450 --> 00:26:45.940
That one thing.

00:26:45.940 --> 00:26:47.911
And yet
they say it didn't hold back lending.

00:26:47.911 --> 00:26:50.026
And that would not have
changed any risk at all for

00:26:50.026 --> 00:26:52.303
banks, for
the most part we don't keep that stuff.

00:26:52.303 --> 00:26:54.170
And it wouldn't have changed the risk for
the government.

00:26:54.170 --> 00:26:57.007
Actually I think the risk is lower
when the economy is stronger.

00:26:57.007 --> 00:26:58.906
And so
that's what they should be doing and

00:26:58.906 --> 00:27:01.130
I think they are going to do
some of that stuff I hope.

00:27:02.520 --> 00:27:06.161
&gt;&gt; Under your management at JP Morgan
come from strength to strength but

00:27:06.161 --> 00:27:09.249
one instance stands out as
a negative during your tenure.

00:27:09.249 --> 00:27:13.550
So, in 2012 a London based trader
lost over $6 billion for the bank.

00:27:13.550 --> 00:27:17.340
Are there any lessons for us all on
how you dealt with that incident and

00:27:17.340 --> 00:27:19.170
do you wish you'd done
anything differently?

00:27:19.170 --> 00:27:21.120
&gt;&gt; Here is an example.

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:23.640
This is what our country has come to,
okay?

00:27:23.640 --> 00:27:26.970
We had a record profit that year.

00:27:26.970 --> 00:27:27.800
We're a big company,

00:27:27.800 --> 00:27:30.740
hugely embarrassed, and I'll tell
you about that mistake in a second.

00:27:30.740 --> 00:27:32.040
No customers were involved.

00:27:33.497 --> 00:27:37.510
Okay, it was originally pronounced
a hedge, obviously it didn't work.

00:27:37.510 --> 00:27:41.930
And yet
we were punished severely in congress.

00:27:41.930 --> 00:27:43.930
$2 billion of fines.

00:27:43.930 --> 00:27:45.540
It's outrageous.

00:27:45.540 --> 00:27:48.130
It's just outrageous that
a company makes a mistake.

00:27:48.130 --> 00:27:49.770
My shareholders got hurt by 6 billion.

00:27:49.770 --> 00:27:51.600
I hurt my shareholders,
I apologize to my shareholders.

00:27:51.600 --> 00:27:55.260
And the government fines me 2 billion
in addition to that, because they can.

00:27:55.260 --> 00:27:58.359
Because they said, it's another example
of a bank being mismanaged etc.

00:27:58.359 --> 00:27:59.454
You know, shit happens!

00:27:59.454 --> 00:28:04.479
[LAUGH] And it was,
we had put on a hedge that was supposed to

00:28:04.479 --> 00:28:10.449
earn a lot in a recession and
basically break even in a normal thing,

00:28:10.449 --> 00:28:14.450
and lose money in a booming environment.

00:28:14.450 --> 00:28:17.750
Think is, we were short high yield and
long investment, something like that.

00:28:17.750 --> 00:28:19.960
And it wasn't the complex
derivatives that mattered.

00:28:19.960 --> 00:28:21.800
Because the fact is that
wasn't the mistake.

00:28:21.800 --> 00:28:26.488
The mistake was when we put thing on,
I thought and

00:28:26.488 --> 00:28:28.960
again I blamed myself and
I went public right away.

00:28:28.960 --> 00:28:31.600
Big mistake, 2 billion,
could be a lot worse.

00:28:31.600 --> 00:28:34.290
Bad strategy, barely vetted,
barely monitored, barely done.

00:28:34.290 --> 00:28:34.910
My fault.

00:28:34.910 --> 00:28:35.496
I'm sorry.

00:28:35.496 --> 00:28:37.290
We're going to fix it.

00:28:37.290 --> 00:28:42.630
And the details in some ways didn't
matter but there was this hugely liquid.

00:28:42.630 --> 00:28:44.780
This typical thing in trading by the way.

00:28:44.780 --> 00:28:48.280
When we had asked them to reduce
the positions in half, I had asked.

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:50.938
I'd looked at some very
simple numbers has asked me.

00:28:50.938 --> 00:28:52.854
I should have asked
a couple more questions.

00:28:52.854 --> 00:28:53.940
They couldn't do it.

00:28:53.940 --> 00:28:58.250
So instead of reducing it in half and
saying we have a problem but if they said

00:28:58.250 --> 00:29:02.050
at that point in time we lost 500 million,
you probably wouldn't have known about it.

00:29:02.050 --> 00:29:03.600
But instead of that,
they kind of doubled down.

00:29:03.600 --> 00:29:06.700
They tried to come up with these old
cockamamie ways to reduce the risk.

00:29:06.700 --> 00:29:08.030
But they just added more and

00:29:08.030 --> 00:29:11.110
more liquid positions on top
of other liquid positions.

00:29:11.110 --> 00:29:14.370
And when you try to unwind that thing,
that's how you get killed.

00:29:14.370 --> 00:29:16.590
But the lesson, there's no lesson to me.

00:29:16.590 --> 00:29:17.770
I knew the second it happened.

00:29:17.770 --> 00:29:18.540
I've seen it before.

00:29:18.540 --> 00:29:20.850
I got the whole Mazarin team together and
all the PR people.

00:29:20.850 --> 00:29:23.070
I've said guys,
they're going to come after us.

00:29:23.070 --> 00:29:25.750
They're going to say Jamie
was like everybody else.

00:29:25.750 --> 00:29:27.510
Big banks are too big to manage.

00:29:27.510 --> 00:29:30.075
Jamie Dimon is a failure
just like these other guys.

00:29:30.075 --> 00:29:33.410
He's off his highly holy horse,
diamonds do break, diamond in the rough.

00:29:33.410 --> 00:29:35.064
I wrote all the headlines.

00:29:35.064 --> 00:29:38.445
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I said forget all of that stuff,

00:29:38.445 --> 00:29:41.140
we're here to serve companies,
serve clients.

00:29:41.140 --> 00:29:43.814
We're going to do our jobs and
hopefully the attack by the, and

00:29:43.814 --> 00:29:47.126
when something like that happens, and
a lot of you have been through this.

00:29:47.126 --> 00:29:51.515
That is a letter to every AG,
to every newspaper, to every senator,

00:29:51.515 --> 00:29:53.040
to every congressman.

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:57.580
And they'll attack like literary
like a bunch of barking hyenas.

00:29:57.580 --> 00:30:00.460
And yet we all look it like
that's okay but it's not okay.

00:30:00.460 --> 00:30:03.700
It's not the way professionals, people
should act when companies have proms and

00:30:03.700 --> 00:30:04.270
stuff like that.

00:30:04.270 --> 00:30:07.920
So the fact is,
there was no great lesson learned.

00:30:07.920 --> 00:30:12.390
Other than follow you, whenever you have
a problem and I say Zuckerberg's got one,

00:30:12.390 --> 00:30:14.970
that's what he going to deal with and I'm
not sure he completely understands that.

00:30:16.220 --> 00:30:20.530
Every AG, every Senator, every
Congressman, they'll turn immediately,

00:30:20.530 --> 00:30:22.810
they'll go on the attack,
they'll take advantage of it.

00:30:22.810 --> 00:30:26.040
I'm surprised they are not talking about
fining them billions of dollars yet.

00:30:26.040 --> 00:30:29.387
And so and that's just society, you just
have to learn how to deal with that stuff.

00:30:29.387 --> 00:30:33.714
So and the biggest risk of all,
the risk committees,

00:30:33.714 --> 00:30:37.800
we obviously bear risk
all around the world.

00:30:37.800 --> 00:30:41.207
That risk committee which was meeting,
had a senior risk officer on it.

00:30:41.207 --> 00:30:44.947
When I asked what about this trade,
they said no,

00:30:44.947 --> 00:30:49.877
that trade was done pretty much
between the bunch of those traders who

00:30:49.877 --> 00:30:54.620
were doing it, and
Ira Sho who's our CIO who I respect.

00:30:54.620 --> 00:30:56.490
They didn't want to share
the risk committee.

00:30:57.710 --> 00:31:00.716
That should never happen,
ever, ever, ever, ever.

00:31:00.716 --> 00:31:03.460
Okay, our risk committees see everything,
my general counsel sees everything,

00:31:03.460 --> 00:31:05.400
my head of compliance sees everything.

00:31:05.400 --> 00:31:07.727
Once someone ever says,
well that we're going to do over here,

00:31:07.727 --> 00:31:09.510
you've got a problem.

00:31:09.510 --> 00:31:13.181
And that tends to create an issue
that will come back and haunt you.

00:31:14.326 --> 00:31:16.570
&gt;&gt; To explore leadership in
tough situations some more,

00:31:16.570 --> 00:31:19.370
the Harvey Weinstein's story is very
much on people's minds at he moment.

00:31:19.370 --> 00:31:22.660
As a leader what do you feel is your role
to play in creating a work culture that is

00:31:22.660 --> 00:31:23.710
very respectful of women?

00:31:23.710 --> 00:31:27.840
&gt;&gt; Well I think it is
your role to do that and.

00:31:27.840 --> 00:31:30.422
And someone who said to me
about that Harvey Weinstein, so

00:31:30.422 --> 00:31:32.050
Wall Street has been that way.

00:31:32.050 --> 00:31:35.950
Wall Street is not that way today folks,
not like that stuff.

00:31:35.950 --> 00:31:38.510
I mean I'm sure there may be harassment
somewhere at J.P. Morgan, but if you think

00:31:38.510 --> 00:31:42.850
it's condoned by anyone at the company
in a senior position, absolutely not!

00:31:42.850 --> 00:31:47.307
Okay and we've got strict processes in
place to look at what people do, and

00:31:47.307 --> 00:31:49.164
how they do it, and review it.

00:31:49.164 --> 00:31:51.310
And if it involved me in any way,
shape, or form,

00:31:51.310 --> 00:31:54.080
it would go directly to
a separate board thing.

00:31:54.080 --> 00:31:56.846
It wouldn't be like I could go
negotiate a settlement on my own or

00:31:56.846 --> 00:31:57.832
something like that.

00:31:57.832 --> 00:32:03.600
So the first thing to Diversity,
and I think it's more important

00:32:03.600 --> 00:32:08.320
than anything else is that everyone in the
company is treated with trust and respect.

00:32:08.320 --> 00:32:11.070
Any meeting you walk in you
feel like you're welcome.

00:32:11.070 --> 00:32:13.253
Anyone can speak up.

00:32:13.253 --> 00:32:15.430
You see the subtle guy thing.

00:32:15.430 --> 00:32:19.340
I've seen this, a senior banker once
told me Jamie, watch this man who runs

00:32:19.340 --> 00:32:22.860
a big part of the company walk into the
partner's room and he'll walk up to you.

00:32:22.860 --> 00:32:24.510
Hey buddy,
how did you like that football game?

00:32:24.510 --> 00:32:26.750
Hey man, what was your score in golf?

00:32:26.750 --> 00:32:29.550
I mean, and he wouldn't even
talk to the women in the room?

00:32:29.550 --> 00:32:30.850
If you're managing a group of people and

00:32:30.850 --> 00:32:33.450
I saw you do that,
you'd get an earful from me.

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:35.320
You've got to talk to everyone.

00:32:35.320 --> 00:32:37.860
You can like some people more than others,
but you've got to talk to everyone.

00:32:37.860 --> 00:32:41.890
And you have to go out of your way to get
the quiet person, the reserved person,

00:32:41.890 --> 00:32:45.352
the person who doesn't want to be
embarrassed to get them to speak up and

00:32:45.352 --> 00:32:46.300
feel comfortable.

00:32:46.300 --> 00:32:50.530
And when you have an environment of
trust and respect, people can shine.

00:32:50.530 --> 00:32:53.691
Okay, now you should have specialized
programs and all that, and I'm for those,

00:32:53.691 --> 00:32:56.010
but they don't work if you
don't create that environment.

00:32:56.010 --> 00:32:57.995
Doesn't matter.

00:32:57.995 --> 00:32:59.520
You just said you would advise it.

00:32:59.520 --> 00:33:00.150
I have three daughters.

00:33:00.150 --> 00:33:02.290
If they were in a terrible environment,
I'd tell them to leave.

00:33:02.290 --> 00:33:05.503
Go find a place that
appreciates your skills.

00:33:05.503 --> 00:33:09.609
And so it will hurt a company ultimately
because if I get to pick my team from

00:33:09.609 --> 00:33:13.540
all people, not just white men,
I would pick a far better team.

00:33:13.540 --> 00:33:15.420
I should point out that at
JP Morgan Chase, and again,

00:33:15.420 --> 00:33:18.360
the press doesn't write this,
half of my direct reports are women.

00:33:19.880 --> 00:33:21.580
Did you hear what I just said?

00:33:21.580 --> 00:33:24.703
Every article, even in
the Wall Street Journal is always saying,

00:33:24.703 --> 00:33:28.955
it'll be 30 years before women represent
30% of the executive suite on Wall Street.

00:33:28.955 --> 00:33:33.756
50% including the head of tech now,
Lori Behr, who's been doing a great job,

00:33:33.756 --> 00:33:35.995
she's been there a couple of months.

00:33:35.995 --> 00:33:42.203
20% of my direct reports are LGBT,
because they're good.

00:33:42.203 --> 00:33:47.386
And 30% of all of the top 200 people in
the company are women, and they run major

00:33:47.386 --> 00:33:53.750
parts of the company, credit card, private
banking, global equity, capital markets.

00:33:53.750 --> 00:33:57.709
An Indian woman is the head of US M&amp;A.

00:33:57.709 --> 00:34:01.540
And that was the most macho belt and
suspenders,

00:34:01.540 --> 00:34:05.192
smoking cigar in the backroom
of all the jobs.

00:34:05.192 --> 00:34:06.157
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:34:06.157 --> 00:34:10.140
her father didn't even want her
to go to school or college.

00:34:10.140 --> 00:34:11.420
She's actually from India.

00:34:11.420 --> 00:34:13.740
She actually Indian, and so, yeah.

00:34:13.740 --> 00:34:16.960
I think if you do it right you'll have
a great environment for everybody.

00:34:16.960 --> 00:34:19.130
And you gotta have your eyes open to it.

00:34:19.130 --> 00:34:24.120
What I was worried about is not,
we have 5,000 branches.

00:34:24.120 --> 00:34:28.400
And I just always know that in
that branch, that guy or woman but

00:34:28.400 --> 00:34:32.620
mostly the guy, can be a bully and
get away with it because we can't see it.

00:34:32.620 --> 00:34:37.110
And no matter what we say, the people
in that branch, they need their job and

00:34:37.110 --> 00:34:38.700
they put up with it.

00:34:38.700 --> 00:34:39.960
Another one you shouldn't allow.

00:34:39.960 --> 00:34:43.650
And I'm sure you've been in companies,
you should never allow, and

00:34:43.650 --> 00:34:46.560
it came to my attention,
you are not allowed to have a golf event,

00:34:46.560 --> 00:34:51.260
invite the men in the division and
not the women, just not allowed.

00:34:51.260 --> 00:34:55.760
My first inclination would be to fire
someone on the spot if I heard about that.

00:34:55.760 --> 00:34:58.280
And don't have golf,
do something different.

00:34:58.280 --> 00:35:00.900
And go out of your way to make
everyone feel accepted, and

00:35:00.900 --> 00:35:02.000
you'll build a great company.

00:35:03.400 --> 00:35:06.550
&gt;&gt; One question we often ask female CEOs
here is how they manage work-life balance.

00:35:06.550 --> 00:35:08.250
We rarely ask male CEOs.

00:35:08.250 --> 00:35:10.880
But I've heard a story that you once
threatened to quit your first job because

00:35:10.880 --> 00:35:13.635
a partner forced you to work
over the weekend unnecessarily.

00:35:13.635 --> 00:35:16.745
We're not suggesting that JP Morgan
investment analysts stop doing the same.

00:35:16.745 --> 00:35:18.990
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But do you have any tips for

00:35:18.990 --> 00:35:21.892
us on structuring our time,
balancing demands made of us, and

00:35:21.892 --> 00:35:22.955
sorting priorities?

00:35:22.955 --> 00:35:25.125
&gt;&gt; When I was an analyst
at this consulting company,

00:35:25.125 --> 00:35:27.305
this consultant had said
you've got to do this work.

00:35:27.305 --> 00:35:29.095
I need it Monday morning, 9 o'clock.

00:35:29.095 --> 00:35:30.364
He gave me the assignment Friday,

00:35:30.364 --> 00:35:32.505
which all the people in
Wall Street complain about.

00:35:32.505 --> 00:35:33.190
The kids are right.

00:35:33.190 --> 00:35:37.458
MDs, they come in, partners, and
they're not thinking about it till Friday.

00:35:37.458 --> 00:35:38.665
And on Friday, I need this.

00:35:38.665 --> 00:35:39.751
I need this, I need it Monday.

00:35:39.751 --> 00:35:40.871
Which means you gotta work all weekend.

00:35:40.871 --> 00:35:43.570
And most of the time you could have
gotten the assignment on Tuesday.

00:35:43.570 --> 00:35:47.056
And so we've actually tried to institute
some policies like you can't give people

00:35:47.056 --> 00:35:50.610
assignments on Friday basically anymore
unless it's approved by someone senior.

00:35:50.610 --> 00:35:54.709
But I worked all weekend and
then I show up at 9 o'clock.

00:35:54.709 --> 00:35:57.746
And I was in the library basically
doing research and stuff like that.

00:35:57.746 --> 00:36:01.492
And he wasn't there and he called in at
12 o'clock or 1 o'clock and said, Robin,

00:36:01.492 --> 00:36:02.820
I worked all weekend.

00:36:02.820 --> 00:36:05.042
You said 9 o'clock, it was critical.

00:36:05.042 --> 00:36:06.870
He said I didn't really
need it until tomorrow.

00:36:06.870 --> 00:36:11.618
I just want to make sure you got it done,
to which I was, bye.

00:36:11.618 --> 00:36:13.340
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:36:13.340 --> 00:36:15.767
I told him I'll never work for you again.

00:36:15.767 --> 00:36:18.275
Now this is not advice, okay?

00:36:18.275 --> 00:36:20.997
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My advice would be a temper is not

00:36:20.997 --> 00:36:21.867
a good thing.

00:36:21.867 --> 00:36:23.762
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:36:23.762 --> 00:36:24.582
&gt;&gt; And they said, well,

00:36:24.582 --> 00:36:27.250
you can't tell the consulting firm
who you're going to work for.

00:36:27.250 --> 00:36:28.915
I said yes, I can,
because they can fire me.

00:36:28.915 --> 00:36:29.770
I was quite serious.

00:36:29.770 --> 00:36:31.510
I never worked for the person again.

00:36:31.510 --> 00:36:33.818
And I think that was a bully thing.

00:36:33.818 --> 00:36:35.914
That was my instinct,
that was just bullying me, and

00:36:35.914 --> 00:36:37.750
it was completely unnecessary.

00:36:37.750 --> 00:36:40.600
And he knew me well enough to know that I
was going to get down when I told them I

00:36:40.600 --> 00:36:42.160
was going to get down stuff like that.

00:36:42.160 --> 00:36:43.713
You can have work-life balance.

00:36:43.713 --> 00:36:47.660
When I tell people at JP Morgan Chase,
it is your job to take care of your mind,

00:36:47.660 --> 00:36:51.377
your body, your spirit, your soul,
your friends, your family, your health.

00:36:51.377 --> 00:36:53.842
Your job, it's not our job.

00:36:53.842 --> 00:36:56.056
So I think there's a little bit
of misunderstanding that some of

00:36:56.056 --> 00:36:56.870
the companies.

00:36:56.870 --> 00:36:58.888
Now we should do everything
we can to help you, right?

00:36:58.888 --> 00:37:04.160
I tell people, we give you medical,
shrinks, Pilates, massages.

00:37:04.160 --> 00:37:07.880
I mean, we really do try to take care
of people, but I can't make up for

00:37:07.880 --> 00:37:09.240
you not doing those other things.

00:37:09.240 --> 00:37:11.080
You really have to do it yourself.

00:37:11.080 --> 00:37:14.080
Most people don't work very smart.

00:37:14.080 --> 00:37:15.402
So first off, work smart.

00:37:15.402 --> 00:37:19.493
I do emails and
phone calls generally once, okay?

00:37:19.493 --> 00:37:24.677
I have three types of reading,
very fast, fast, very slow.

00:37:24.677 --> 00:37:27.060
So the very slow is the complex.

00:37:27.060 --> 00:37:28.030
I really need to think about it.

00:37:28.030 --> 00:37:29.878
Very fast might be a magazine.

00:37:29.878 --> 00:37:31.972
I just want to see if there is
something interesting in there or

00:37:31.972 --> 00:37:32.747
something like that.

00:37:32.747 --> 00:37:34.350
People don't run their lives efficiently.

00:37:34.350 --> 00:37:37.840
And I'm sure you've seen even in
school here, people are frenzied,

00:37:37.840 --> 00:37:38.910
they're always frenzied.

00:37:38.910 --> 00:37:41.040
They're late, they can't get back,
I'm sorry I didn't get back to you,

00:37:41.040 --> 00:37:42.150
I couldn't get this done.

00:37:42.150 --> 00:37:44.130
I got my cases, I couldn't do this.

00:37:44.130 --> 00:37:47.029
If you're frenzied, it's you.

00:37:47.029 --> 00:37:48.858
It's not the school, it's not the job,

00:37:48.858 --> 00:37:50.955
it's you because a lot
of us aren't frenzied.

00:37:50.955 --> 00:37:56.200
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm never late, almost never late.

00:37:56.200 --> 00:37:57.076
I'm not frenzied.

00:37:57.076 --> 00:37:58.160
I have a nice.

00:37:58.160 --> 00:38:00.310
I know exactly what people are doing.

00:38:00.310 --> 00:38:05.440
I schedule time for myself for exercise,
and for making my phone calls.

00:38:05.440 --> 00:38:06.950
And if I didn't do it this morning,

00:38:06.950 --> 00:38:09.820
I would feel harried if
I wasn't all caught up.

00:38:11.280 --> 00:38:12.590
So work really smart.

00:38:12.590 --> 00:38:14.110
Learn how to work smart.

00:38:14.110 --> 00:38:18.030
And then, in [INAUDIBLE] I think it's
really difficult when you have children.

00:38:18.030 --> 00:38:19.860
That does burn the candle at both ends.

00:38:19.860 --> 00:38:23.720
My advice to men, is you better drop
watching every football game and

00:38:23.720 --> 00:38:26.160
playing golf on both Saturday and Sunday.

00:38:26.160 --> 00:38:28.270
Do something with your kids.

00:38:28.270 --> 00:38:29.100
If you don't, it's neglect.

00:38:30.220 --> 00:38:32.030
Okay, you call it whatever you want, but

00:38:32.030 --> 00:38:34.970
I have a lot of friends complain
that they have no family time.

00:38:34.970 --> 00:38:36.310
But they go play golf on Saturday and
Sunday.

00:38:36.310 --> 00:38:39.390
I'm playing golf on Saturday and
Sunday means you wake up at 7, right?

00:38:39.390 --> 00:38:41.143
You go play golf for three hours.

00:38:41.143 --> 00:38:45.166
You have lunch, usually a Bloody Mary,
you go home and take a nap.

00:38:45.166 --> 00:38:47.630
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay, and

00:38:47.630 --> 00:38:49.379
then you go to dinner with friends.

00:38:49.379 --> 00:38:51.140
You should have done
something with your family.

00:38:51.140 --> 00:38:53.461
And so you can do it.

00:38:53.461 --> 00:38:55.810
And if you're a woman,
I think it's even harder.

00:38:55.810 --> 00:38:58.600
And I think there's some jobs that
are really conducive to helping you, and

00:38:58.600 --> 00:38:59.930
some aren't.

00:38:59.930 --> 00:39:01.300
And you gotta think that
through a little bit.

00:39:01.300 --> 00:39:04.360
And one of the greatest speech I ever saw
a woman give was, you can have it all,

00:39:04.360 --> 00:39:06.040
you just can't have it
all at the same time.

00:39:06.040 --> 00:39:07.970
And just be a little thoughtful
how you manage your life.

00:39:07.970 --> 00:39:10.518
That could be a man who's gotta stay
at home to take care of kids, or

00:39:10.518 --> 00:39:12.320
sick parents or something like that.

00:39:12.320 --> 00:39:15.680
And so it's a long race and
you can pretty much do it all.

00:39:15.680 --> 00:39:19.890
But you gotta make sure you save time for
yourself to do those things.

00:39:19.890 --> 00:39:21.523
And even the toughest job you can do that.

00:39:23.038 --> 00:39:26.405
&gt;&gt; In 2014 you continued to actively
lead JP Morgan despite battling and

00:39:26.405 --> 00:39:28.170
defeating throat cancer.

00:39:28.170 --> 00:39:31.420
Many people were incredibly impressed by
your decision to stay at the helm with

00:39:31.420 --> 00:39:32.620
the company during that time.

00:39:32.620 --> 00:39:35.860
What continues to motivate you
under those circumstances?

00:39:35.860 --> 00:39:39.140
&gt;&gt; Well, it's not up to me to
stand down with the company.

00:39:39.140 --> 00:39:43.205
You're never quite over
with cancer either.

00:39:43.205 --> 00:39:44.530
At first, I had to go public with it.

00:39:44.530 --> 00:39:47.410
It wasn't my preference, but
I couldn't be seen walking in and

00:39:47.410 --> 00:39:49.400
out of Sloan Kettering.

00:39:49.400 --> 00:39:51.140
And of course I was going to lose weight.

00:39:51.140 --> 00:39:53.700
I didn't lose my hair, but
I could have, and all that.

00:39:53.700 --> 00:39:55.960
So I just went completely clean.

00:39:55.960 --> 00:39:58.540
I did go to work, almost every day.

00:39:58.540 --> 00:40:01.070
But that does not mean I worked every day.

00:40:01.070 --> 00:40:05.190
I mean I was taking four or five naps,
I lost like 40 pounds and I didn't want to

00:40:05.190 --> 00:40:08.170
get close to anyone because your
immune system is virtually gone.

00:40:08.170 --> 00:40:10.700
So I didn't even want to
get a cold at that point.

00:40:10.700 --> 00:40:15.140
But people say [INAUDIBLE] so
I was sitting at home and

00:40:15.140 --> 00:40:19.090
I go get my treatment every morning
radiation and then I'd go home.

00:40:19.090 --> 00:40:22.820
I'd try to exercise, I went from
running five miles to walking five, to

00:40:22.820 --> 00:40:27.570
literally walking for a minute, going on
the floor because I was going to pass out,

00:40:27.570 --> 00:40:30.040
walking a minute, but
it made me feel better.

00:40:30.040 --> 00:40:33.070
But if I stayed at home
you know what I was doing?

00:40:33.070 --> 00:40:35.310
I couldn't read I thought I
could read eight hours a day but

00:40:35.310 --> 00:40:38.845
I just couldn't do it but I was staying
home I was watching ISIS and Ebola.

00:40:38.845 --> 00:40:43.360
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And when I go to the office,

00:40:43.360 --> 00:40:47.840
I'm surrounded by friends and
interesting things and you keep mind well.

00:40:47.840 --> 00:40:51.320
I had some meeting and
so it was better for me.

00:40:51.320 --> 00:40:53.500
And the board was fully
beefed the whole time and

00:40:53.500 --> 00:40:55.800
I have unbelievable management team and
they were great.

00:40:55.800 --> 00:40:59.470
And my family is great and so it was

00:40:59.470 --> 00:41:02.750
in some ways a heart warming experience
other than the fact that it's terrifying.

00:41:03.810 --> 00:41:07.236
&gt;&gt; Jamie thank you so much, I think we'll
now turn it over to the audience for Q&amp;A.

00:41:15.760 --> 00:41:17.730
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much for
being here Mister Diamond.

00:41:17.730 --> 00:41:21.850
At what level do you think
BitCoin has to get to before

00:41:23.430 --> 00:41:26.710
JP Morgan would embrace it and
at what level of demand?

00:41:26.710 --> 00:41:27.790
You said there is a level and

00:41:27.790 --> 00:41:30.930
I'm wondering what that would be as look
today at the price of $7,000 a coin.

00:41:30.930 --> 00:41:33.540
&gt;&gt; I doubt we're going to
embrace it at any price.

00:41:35.060 --> 00:41:38.290
So I mean to me it's
that I'm worried about

00:41:38.290 --> 00:41:42.920
being caught up in this maelstrom of
people losing money, and stuff like that,

00:41:42.920 --> 00:41:45.320
there's no reason for
us to do anything with Bitcoin.

00:41:45.320 --> 00:41:48.540
Now, if you're a client, and
you're going to trade it on the CMU,

00:41:48.540 --> 00:41:52.790
or something like that, I'd advise you
not to do it, but should we do it and

00:41:52.790 --> 00:41:55.180
put in trades for a client who asked us,
who is sophisticated,

00:41:55.180 --> 00:41:58.150
and obviously not bright,
but sophisticated.

00:41:58.150 --> 00:41:59.990
Maybe, I don't know.

00:41:59.990 --> 00:42:03.870
I mean and I told the senior team
I don't tell them what to do,

00:42:03.870 --> 00:42:07.283
I told to place a note to Daniel and

00:42:07.283 --> 00:42:11.562
I said Daniel I know you heard what
I said, it's your job to what you do.

00:42:11.562 --> 00:42:13.880
And if you disagree with
me that's completely fine.

00:42:13.880 --> 00:42:16.322
I'll probably do whatever he wants,
inspite what I said.

00:42:20.055 --> 00:42:22.980
Hi, hello, hi thanks for coming.

00:42:24.250 --> 00:42:26.840
One thing, I'm I'm a second year MBA.

00:42:26.840 --> 00:42:30.210
A couple things you said that
were quite interesting were that

00:42:30.210 --> 00:42:33.360
there is no lesson to be learned,
and I don't care if I'm wrong.

00:42:33.360 --> 00:42:36.490
And I guess from a lot of senior leaders
I've noticed that self-awareness and

00:42:36.490 --> 00:42:39.820
learning from mistakes or
key characteristics?

00:42:39.820 --> 00:42:40.900
Do you agree, and if so,

00:42:40.900 --> 00:42:45.220
do you have an example of a time when
you've actually tried to build those?

00:42:45.220 --> 00:42:47.140
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so
I did mention mistakes I've made.

00:42:47.140 --> 00:42:48.920
I should have asked some more questions.

00:42:48.920 --> 00:42:52.280
There's one question I would've known.

00:42:52.280 --> 00:42:53.270
Just one.

00:42:53.270 --> 00:42:55.950
There's the one where I said
it was a tempest in a teapot.

00:42:55.950 --> 00:42:57.200
I asked the question.

00:42:57.200 --> 00:42:58.320
I got the wrong answer.

00:42:58.320 --> 00:42:59.650
It was, how liquid are the positions?

00:42:59.650 --> 00:43:01.200
How many days can you get out?

00:43:01.200 --> 00:43:01.878
I was told ten.

00:43:03.979 --> 00:43:07.520
Yeah, it was like hundreds and
the second I heard that, I said, shit.

00:43:07.520 --> 00:43:09.350
We have a real problem.

00:43:09.350 --> 00:43:09.970
So you learn.
And

00:43:09.970 --> 00:43:11.950
I actually wrote about it
in my chairman's lever.

00:43:11.950 --> 00:43:14.120
I said, lessons learned or relearned.

00:43:14.120 --> 00:43:14.650
I knew that.

00:43:14.650 --> 00:43:17.830
It's like you kind of sometimes learn
the same lessons over and over.

00:43:17.830 --> 00:43:20.450
It's like being in a boxing ring,
They say duck, hold your hands up.

00:43:20.450 --> 00:43:24.300
Yeah, well,
That's harder to do than you think.

00:43:24.300 --> 00:43:28.350
So you do learn, you do get better as you
get older in management and people and

00:43:28.350 --> 00:43:29.900
decision making.

00:43:29.900 --> 00:43:33.390
But I'll tell you what the biggest
mistakes I've made are.

00:43:33.390 --> 00:43:36.070
They're not the specific one
like the London Whale but

00:43:36.070 --> 00:43:37.570
more the patterns around it.

00:43:37.570 --> 00:43:39.590
Temper's a bad idea.

00:43:39.590 --> 00:43:41.040
Any decision made out of temper,

00:43:41.040 --> 00:43:45.210
even treating people with temper
you They're always being careful.

00:43:45.210 --> 00:43:48.240
While you walk in, which mood is he in,
and stuff like that.

00:43:48.240 --> 00:43:49.770
You don't get the best out of people.

00:43:49.770 --> 00:43:53.410
And sometimes earlier in my life, it was
said, it was a well placed blow of anger.

00:43:53.410 --> 00:43:54.160
No, it wasn't.

00:43:54.160 --> 00:43:56.580
It was just a welling up of anger.

00:43:56.580 --> 00:43:59.030
It usually clouded your judgement,
as opposed to.

00:43:59.030 --> 00:44:04.180
So, I knew I'd finally matured
when about 10 years ago, I said,

00:44:04.180 --> 00:44:06.650
someone was sitting in front
of me talking about something.

00:44:06.650 --> 00:44:08.690
It was about investment banker comp.

00:44:08.690 --> 00:44:10.760
And I was getting so mad.

00:44:10.760 --> 00:44:11.620
And I said, you know what?

00:44:12.920 --> 00:44:14.220
I'm going to leave the room.

00:44:14.220 --> 00:44:15.790
You guys stay here for ten minutes.

00:44:15.790 --> 00:44:16.460
I'm going to come back.

00:44:16.460 --> 00:44:17.490
I went out.

00:44:17.490 --> 00:44:19.540
I thought about what made me mad.

00:44:19.540 --> 00:44:20.200
I came back.

00:44:20.200 --> 00:44:25.140
I told them very politely How offensive
what they were saying was to me, etc.

00:44:25.140 --> 00:44:31.030
But other mistakes, when you don't get the
right people involved, plain and simple.

00:44:31.030 --> 00:44:32.970
Sometimes the answer is
waiting to be found.

00:44:32.970 --> 00:44:35.110
You just have to get the people
in the room to work it.

00:44:35.110 --> 00:44:37.300
Making decisions when you're tired.

00:44:37.300 --> 00:44:38.977
Making decisions on Friday.

00:44:41.582 --> 00:44:44.427
Not throughly vetting people, the biggest
mistakes you make are people mistakes and

00:44:44.427 --> 00:44:45.450
I've made plenty.

00:44:45.450 --> 00:44:48.370
The people mistakes really
set back an organization

00:44:48.370 --> 00:44:49.970
because it takes a long
time to figure it out.

00:44:49.970 --> 00:44:52.450
And a lot of damage can be done,
then you have to replace them, so

00:44:52.450 --> 00:44:53.410
it's like a year and a half.

00:44:53.410 --> 00:44:56.520
A person mistake takes a year and
a half of setting the company back.

00:44:56.520 --> 00:44:58.040
And you may lose good
people in the meantime

00:44:58.040 --> 00:44:59.970
because when you put a person
in that job they often

00:44:59.970 --> 00:45:02.930
drive out some other good people by the
time you figured out you made a mistake.

00:45:02.930 --> 00:45:06.590
And you can avoid that because
I dont know your name but

00:45:06.590 --> 00:45:09.510
I can get the whole book on you and
never meet you.

00:45:09.510 --> 00:45:11.540
Okay I can find out how smart you are,
how you treat people.

00:45:11.540 --> 00:45:14.600
How you treat your friends, bosses, peers,
subordinates, whether you're on time,

00:45:14.600 --> 00:45:17.620
whether you show up, whether people
trust you, whether you shave the truth.

00:45:17.620 --> 00:45:19.270
I get that whole dossier on you.

00:45:19.270 --> 00:45:22.220
So we're pretty good now at making
sure when we put people on these big

00:45:22.220 --> 00:45:23.790
jobs that we know they're pretty good.

00:45:23.790 --> 00:45:25.210
You still can make a mistake, but

00:45:25.210 --> 00:45:28.986
you'd be surprised, the error rate is just
dramatically less than it ever was before.

00:45:32.195 --> 00:45:33.574
Hello.

00:45:33.574 --> 00:45:35.680
Hi.
I'm a 2018 Sloan fellow.

00:45:35.680 --> 00:45:40.000
I was working for JP Morgan in London
up until March of this year and

00:45:40.000 --> 00:45:44.500
I was just wondering where do you see the
future of European financial services and

00:45:44.500 --> 00:45:46.640
particularly JP Morgan in
the context of Brexit?

00:45:47.660 --> 00:45:52.580
So Brexit I mean the fact is we're
going to be prepared for hard Brexit

00:45:52.580 --> 00:45:55.790
not because we're predicting, though I
think it is pretty likely at this point.

00:45:55.790 --> 00:45:59.410
All right I think it's a very complex
thing, it's not great for Brittain.

00:45:59.410 --> 00:46:03.980
But the fact is to be prepared all we have
to do is have our European subsidiaries

00:46:03.980 --> 00:46:05.490
get more capital, certain licenses,

00:46:05.490 --> 00:46:07.160
certain approvals which
we're going to get.

00:46:07.160 --> 00:46:13.550
So if you're had the hard Brexit it
defaults the next day the existing EU law.

00:46:13.550 --> 00:46:17.540
Under existing EU law we simply have
to do certain things contractually

00:46:17.540 --> 00:46:21.310
in sovereign in entities in the EU.

00:46:21.310 --> 00:46:27.000
And we have them we've got licences,
proper subsidiaries and In Germany,

00:46:27.000 --> 00:46:33.640
Ireland, Brussels, it's only about 500
jobs, so it's not that big a deal, but in

00:46:33.640 --> 00:46:38.560
reality 12,000 people are servicing Europe
out of London, even after a hard Brexit.

00:46:38.560 --> 00:46:42.950
And the real issue is what happens
to the 12,000 in the 10 years after?

00:46:42.950 --> 00:46:45.920
Because I know it's going to
happen Regulators and

00:46:45.920 --> 00:46:48.730
politicians are going to walk in and
say we're uncomfortable.

00:46:48.730 --> 00:46:49.940
We know you did the license and

00:46:49.940 --> 00:46:52.930
you booked the trade of this subsidiary
but you know back to back to that

00:46:52.930 --> 00:46:57.450
subsidiary to risk manage it where you
have the banker do the ECM deal here but

00:46:57.450 --> 00:47:02.880
the research, risk, compliance, audit,
trading was all done in London.

00:47:02.880 --> 00:47:06.000
And that's not sufficient we don't want to
pretend we can grow this type of business

00:47:06.000 --> 00:47:06.860
you gotta move it here.

00:47:06.860 --> 00:47:10.260
I think there's going to be huge pressure
moving those 12,000 people over time

00:47:10.260 --> 00:47:12.430
into the EU.

00:47:12.430 --> 00:47:15.550
And my goal is to make sure we
conserve our clients none stop.

00:47:15.550 --> 00:47:18.010
It will cost us more money
it's a real pain in the ass.

00:47:18.010 --> 00:47:19.880
But that's not the point
I am worried about it.

00:47:19.880 --> 00:47:21.740
I want to be able to serve
those European clients.

00:47:21.740 --> 00:47:25.797
And we bank every European country just
about Every big European institution,

00:47:25.797 --> 00:47:29.189
every big European investor that's
what I gotta worry about being

00:47:29.189 --> 00:47:31.017
prepared to do that on the next day.

00:47:33.957 --> 00:47:38.531
&gt;&gt; Thank you this is a continuation
of what you're talking about, but

00:47:38.531 --> 00:47:44.050
what areas of the bank do you see most
exposed to technology disruption?

00:47:44.050 --> 00:47:46.320
You mentioned sales and
trading before Yeah so

00:47:46.320 --> 00:47:50.070
first of all like I said technology has
been changing the world forever, so

00:47:50.070 --> 00:47:51.740
it's not like it's completely different.

00:47:51.740 --> 00:47:55.250
I think it's faster now,
more money is going into fintech.

00:47:55.250 --> 00:47:58.550
Agile technology can do things
that couldn't be done before.

00:47:58.550 --> 00:48:01.340
And I think it's incompetent upon any
business to use technology to do a better

00:48:01.340 --> 00:48:02.300
for its client.

00:48:02.300 --> 00:48:04.620
Better could be better, faster, cheaper,

00:48:04.620 --> 00:48:08.430
straight through processing obviously
making a digital mobile, stuff like that.

00:48:08.430 --> 00:48:09.850
I remember banks have been doing that for
years and

00:48:09.850 --> 00:48:12.800
you can buy and sell effects on
our on your mobile phone now.

00:48:12.800 --> 00:48:16.595
And when some trade,
some trader did a $400 million trade and

00:48:16.595 --> 00:48:19.910
I said, God,
$400 million on a mobile phone.

00:48:19.910 --> 00:48:22.620
I said we've gotta put
breathalyzers on those phones.

00:48:22.620 --> 00:48:26.533
I just envisioned some macho trader
having two martinis and saying,

00:48:26.533 --> 00:48:27.687
I'm going to do this.

00:48:27.687 --> 00:48:28.580
But you know what?

00:48:28.580 --> 00:48:32.420
When I mentioned that to the risk
people they said, we do have that.

00:48:32.420 --> 00:48:34.960
And we can actually put that in, so that
you can't do a trade without [INAUDIBLE]

00:48:34.960 --> 00:48:36.510
breathalyzer or something like.

00:48:36.510 --> 00:48:37.010
Anyway.

00:48:38.420 --> 00:48:39.710
So I think, in some ways,

00:48:39.710 --> 00:48:42.020
always look at a business from
the standpoint of the client.

00:48:42.020 --> 00:48:43.550
So forget J.P. Morgan.

00:48:43.550 --> 00:48:47.200
A lot of the things the clients need,
they're still going to need.

00:48:47.200 --> 00:48:49.070
In fact, they'll need you to go overtime.

00:48:49.070 --> 00:48:51.170
ECM will still be there,
DCM will be still there.

00:48:51.170 --> 00:48:53.520
Hedging FX and hedging interest
rates will still be there.

00:48:53.520 --> 00:48:56.440
M&amp;A advice will still be there, but
we have to use technology to do

00:48:56.440 --> 00:48:58.850
things better, faster,
quicker like everybody else.

00:48:58.850 --> 00:49:01.170
And straight through and
all that kind of stuff.

00:49:01.170 --> 00:49:03.020
But you ask me the thing that's
going to be most disrupted,

00:49:03.020 --> 00:49:05.280
it's going to be payments.

00:49:05.280 --> 00:49:07.190
There are payment systems
that you're integrating.

00:49:07.190 --> 00:49:09.602
All the HCh Systems, terrible.

00:49:09.602 --> 00:49:11.266
But we just put out a P2P.

00:49:11.266 --> 00:49:12.220
You all use Venmo?

00:49:12.220 --> 00:49:14.910
We just put out a P2P, it's realtime.

00:49:14.910 --> 00:49:16.110
Bank to bank.

00:49:16.110 --> 00:49:19.080
It's safer, it's better,
it's realtime, it's not social yet.

00:49:19.080 --> 00:49:22.766
So I don't possibly understand why
you'd want to share all that stuff with

00:49:22.766 --> 00:49:26.650
your friends, but could be that,
it could be in wholesale payments.

00:49:26.650 --> 00:49:29.087
But you could go on screens now
move money around the world,

00:49:29.087 --> 00:49:30.561
convert to different currencies.

00:49:30.561 --> 00:49:31.873
And so we're doing it, but

00:49:31.873 --> 00:49:35.090
if you ask me that's where most
of stuff going to take place.

00:49:35.090 --> 00:49:38.120
Remember, we've invested in
100 Fintech companies and

00:49:38.120 --> 00:49:41.930
we're going to compete,
build, buy, whatever it is.

00:49:41.930 --> 00:49:45.315
And just give you an example,
we're going to have online trading and

00:49:45.315 --> 00:49:47.150
mobile investing for our clients.

00:49:47.150 --> 00:49:49.900
It's in beta today,
I'm not sure I'm going to roll it out.

00:49:49.900 --> 00:49:53.140
We're going to roll it out and test it and
test price and all the stuffs like that,

00:49:53.140 --> 00:49:55.710
but we can analog those things for
free if we wanted to.

00:49:55.710 --> 00:49:58.560
So we're not, we're not dead in the water.

00:49:58.560 --> 00:50:01.290
So when the time comes, you are going
to see us being very aggressive to win

00:50:01.290 --> 00:50:02.340
businesses like we always have.

00:50:05.900 --> 00:50:10.000
We rolled out, it's not being marketed,
we rolled out a mobile-only bank in St.

00:50:10.000 --> 00:50:12.020
Louis recently and
we are going to test that.

00:50:12.020 --> 00:50:15.450
More for millennials than anyone else, but
from opening the account to moving money,

00:50:15.450 --> 00:50:17.030
it's only a debit card.

00:50:17.030 --> 00:50:19.579
And we're going to see how that works and

00:50:19.579 --> 00:50:24.131
then we are going to try to modify
that and then do it in all 50 states.

00:50:24.131 --> 00:50:25.150
Go ahead.

00:50:25.150 --> 00:50:26.680
They'll get it eventually.

00:50:26.680 --> 00:50:28.620
No, hold it up.

00:50:28.620 --> 00:50:31.932
&gt;&gt; Jonathan Walden,
third year PhD student now and

00:50:31.932 --> 00:50:35.808
I have a question about how
you brought up bureaucracy and

00:50:35.808 --> 00:50:40.725
politicalization, and
the detriments that does to the firm.

00:50:40.725 --> 00:50:45.835
And my concern is how do you assess
the bureaucratization within J.P.

00:50:45.835 --> 00:50:47.645
Morgan, compared with your peers.

00:50:47.645 --> 00:50:52.155
And how do you trade off the agency that
you give to individual portfolio managers

00:50:52.155 --> 00:50:55.010
with the oversight from compliance?

00:50:55.010 --> 00:50:59.880
Well, we're pretty rigorous how we run
the company, so we meet all the time, and

00:50:59.880 --> 00:51:04.450
go through everything top to bottom, and
we raises issues and stuff like that.

00:51:04.450 --> 00:51:07.640
So you know who your authorities
are if you're a trader.

00:51:07.640 --> 00:51:09.010
If you're a bank, you know exactly.

00:51:09.010 --> 00:51:11.750
You also know he's not supposed to do,
and you also when

00:51:11.750 --> 00:51:14.160
you go to compliance committee, risk
committee, an underwriting committee, or

00:51:14.160 --> 00:51:16.230
a credit committee,
there's no mystery to any of that.

00:51:16.230 --> 00:51:18.700
And it happens every day and
we barely make mistakes.

00:51:18.700 --> 00:51:21.800
And I'm not going to stand behind the
regulatory things, and we have a thousand

00:51:21.800 --> 00:51:25.200
new rules and regular requirements and
that does create some of the bureaucracy,

00:51:25.200 --> 00:51:28.190
but it's not an excuse for us to say,
this is a better way to do it.

00:51:28.190 --> 00:51:30.710
We listen to customers all the time.

00:51:30.710 --> 00:51:35.890
The management team is open, every issue,
the agenda is always set by them.

00:51:35.890 --> 00:51:37.530
So it isn't like you walk in the room and
I set the agenda.

00:51:37.530 --> 00:51:41.010
If you work for me, I expect you
to raise everything important.

00:51:41.010 --> 00:51:44.270
Everything and
anything including bureaucracies,

00:51:44.270 --> 00:51:48.310
cost slow down, bad service,
competitors got something better than us.

00:51:48.310 --> 00:51:51.080
That's your job not my job and, so I mean,

00:51:51.080 --> 00:51:53.740
I do it with you hoping presumably you're
going to do it with your management team.

00:51:53.740 --> 00:51:55.540
So you're always airing all this issues.

00:51:55.540 --> 00:51:58.600
We go on bus trips, we go to call centers,
we go to op centers, we got client

00:51:58.600 --> 00:52:03.510
reviews, we got advisory groups, and all
those things get circulated all of them.

00:52:03.510 --> 00:52:06.430
So all the complaints we're
always trying to deal with that

00:52:06.430 --> 00:52:08.120
become better and faster.

00:52:08.120 --> 00:52:10.940
I think one of the tough
things about management is

00:52:10.940 --> 00:52:12.570
you have to get rid of the bad people.

00:52:13.840 --> 00:52:16.790
So I can say anything I want,
but if I have a bunch of goomas,

00:52:16.790 --> 00:52:21.020
or they're not that good, or
they're friends of Jamie type of thing.

00:52:21.020 --> 00:52:23.100
That is what caused the problem.

00:52:23.100 --> 00:52:26.410
And so very well from people who were
on going to get rid of the bad people.

00:52:26.410 --> 00:52:29.743
Bad could be, because their culture is
bad and also because they're not good.

00:52:29.743 --> 00:52:31.928
They're not good enough.

00:52:31.928 --> 00:52:35.480
So in meritocracy people say,
Joe deserves the job or

00:52:35.480 --> 00:52:37.950
good old Joe and spending a long time.

00:52:37.950 --> 00:52:39.170
That doesn't mean they deserve the job.

00:52:40.210 --> 00:52:42.880
And in sports if you're not batting
250 you're not going to be playing

00:52:42.880 --> 00:52:44.200
second base.

00:52:44.200 --> 00:52:46.840
And it's very easy, you take out
a pitcher that's not doing a good job.

00:52:46.840 --> 00:52:48.600
In business there are left
in those jobs a long time.

00:52:48.600 --> 00:52:50.870
I'm not saying treat them disrespectfully.

00:52:50.870 --> 00:52:53.110
If you treat people disrespectfully,

00:52:53.110 --> 00:52:55.190
you're going to lose their hearts and
minds too.

00:52:55.190 --> 00:52:58.290
So it's gotta be a very respectful,
we're going to make a change for

00:52:58.290 --> 00:52:59.600
the following reasons.

00:52:59.600 --> 00:53:00.200
But you really,

00:53:00.200 --> 00:53:04.310
if you're going to run a real meritocracy,
you've gotta make sure you really do it.

00:53:04.310 --> 00:53:08.060
And those people, it should
filter through the whole company.

00:53:08.060 --> 00:53:10.730
Loyalty is such a misnamed
thing sometimes.

00:53:10.730 --> 00:53:13.740
What they usually mean is, I was asked
when I was a young CEO in a room like

00:53:13.740 --> 00:53:15.210
this, we had just done a big deal,

00:53:15.210 --> 00:53:17.840
how can we be loyal to you
when you weren't loyal to Joe?

00:53:17.840 --> 00:53:18.510
He's been around.

00:53:18.510 --> 00:53:21.190
He trained a lot of people in the room and
you just demoted him.

00:53:21.190 --> 00:53:22.340
I couldn't answer the question.

00:53:22.340 --> 00:53:24.660
And I called the person the next day, and

00:53:24.660 --> 00:53:26.160
I answer every question
eventually by the way.

00:53:26.160 --> 00:53:29.180
But I called him the next day and
I said, I thought about it and

00:53:29.180 --> 00:53:30.550
you got it backwards.

00:53:30.550 --> 00:53:33.110
If I was loyal to Joe and
kept him in the job.

00:53:33.110 --> 00:53:35.550
And most people thought he just
wasn't doing a good job anymore.

00:53:35.550 --> 00:53:36.620
Who am I being disloyal to?

00:53:37.920 --> 00:53:40.319
Everybody else and the customer.

00:53:40.319 --> 00:53:42.011
And so it's a hard thing to do but and

00:53:42.011 --> 00:53:45.150
that's the hardest thing to
do in management right there.

00:53:45.150 --> 00:53:48.110
And a lot of you will fail doing it,
because you deal the thin hams in

00:53:48.110 --> 00:53:51.930
management, you're owned by this desk down
the hall and they all take care of it.

00:53:51.930 --> 00:53:54.550
Don't worry I'll take care of it and
basically that becomes a club.

00:53:55.790 --> 00:53:58.160
And they're not taking care of it for
the company or the client.

00:53:58.160 --> 00:54:00.280
They're taking care of it generally for
themselves.

00:54:00.280 --> 00:54:03.570
And so you have to be very, very careful
in management, why you're doing what

00:54:03.570 --> 00:54:07.270
you're doing, and who gets to speak up and
who's in the room and things like that.

00:54:08.880 --> 00:54:10.033
&gt;&gt; We got time for one more question.

00:54:13.212 --> 00:54:14.204
&gt;&gt; So I worked for J.P.

00:54:14.204 --> 00:54:18.040
Morgan Chase back in 2012, part of
the team that redesigned the chase.com.

00:54:18.040 --> 00:54:22.450
So I do know Chase is always more towards
the technically advanced company.

00:54:22.450 --> 00:54:25.070
And this question is more
toward the tech side.

00:54:25.070 --> 00:54:29.780
What is the stand of backing community
in AI, artificial intelligence?

00:54:29.780 --> 00:54:32.370
Like AI is going to take
over automation stuff.

00:54:32.370 --> 00:54:33.250
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And what does

00:54:33.250 --> 00:54:34.740
banking community thinks about it?

00:54:34.740 --> 00:54:36.950
&gt;&gt; Well,
I don't care what banking community think,

00:54:36.950 --> 00:54:38.381
I'll tell you what I think.

00:54:38.381 --> 00:54:43.710
[LAUGH] Big data, AI, machine learning,
chat boxes, roblox, they're all real.

00:54:43.710 --> 00:54:47.940
We have thousands in data scientists and
PhDs and not just coding.

00:54:47.940 --> 00:54:51.935
And we spent $9 billion a year in
technology, $700 million in cyber,

00:54:51.935 --> 00:54:54.350
probably $1 billion a year now and

00:54:54.350 --> 00:54:57.140
just some of these stuff just
uniquely devoted to that.

00:54:57.140 --> 00:55:00.518
And it is real it is an amazing thing
that you can do, that you can do today in

00:55:00.518 --> 00:55:03.406
a split second so we just to,
when you swiped that credit card,

00:55:03.406 --> 00:55:06.350
it goes through hundreds maybe
a thousand algorithms to test for

00:55:06.350 --> 00:55:08.497
fraud how you did it where
you did it what time.

00:55:08.497 --> 00:55:10.680
We do the same thing for big payments.

00:55:10.680 --> 00:55:13.030
So if you run a company,
small business large company,

00:55:13.030 --> 00:55:15.610
like what happened to the Federal Reserve
never happened to us.

00:55:15.610 --> 00:55:17.330
Payments at the wrong time,
different type,

00:55:17.330 --> 00:55:20.360
different entry, we're actually
monitoring your systems all the time.

00:55:20.360 --> 00:55:23.580
If we see anything wrong we're calling
you up and stuff like that, so it's real.

00:55:23.580 --> 00:55:27.250
The hardest part about it isn't
understanding it or knowing

00:55:27.250 --> 00:55:30.770
the technology, it's getting the people in
the company to understand the power of it.

00:55:30.770 --> 00:55:35.670
So when we first started big data, and big
data and we put it up at the central for

00:55:35.670 --> 00:55:37.500
standards, and we used Hadoop.

00:55:37.500 --> 00:55:40.050
To make sure that you don't end up
with 84 different platforms and

00:55:40.050 --> 00:55:42.010
stuff like that out of control.

00:55:42.010 --> 00:55:45.610
And machine learning has another problem
with regulation that I won't go through

00:55:45.610 --> 00:55:47.200
but we put it at the top.

00:55:47.200 --> 00:55:50.130
And we had to say, hey,
you do prospecting, how do you do it,

00:55:50.130 --> 00:55:50.960
why do you do it?

00:55:50.960 --> 00:55:53.839
We made prospecting ten time more
efficient in a commercial bank.

00:55:55.410 --> 00:56:00.260
Ten times and don't think, okay, the cost
is lower, instead of $30,000 it's $3,000,

00:56:00.260 --> 00:56:02.970
it also means we can put two
more bankers in Grand Rapids.

00:56:04.460 --> 00:56:06.242
It's a huge benefit to the company, and

00:56:06.242 --> 00:56:09.452
be immediately productive as opposed
to take three years to build it up.

00:56:09.452 --> 00:56:14.017
And then once we did that,
we now have it in every division.

00:56:14.017 --> 00:56:17.450
So every division has a big data,
AI, machine learning thing.

00:56:17.450 --> 00:56:20.156
And we ask every manager, come back and

00:56:20.156 --> 00:56:23.604
give us the use case that
you think is best for you.

00:56:23.604 --> 00:56:24.817
The demand is going up like this.

00:56:24.817 --> 00:56:26.220
But it's one of the areas
you want the demand.

00:56:27.260 --> 00:56:28.930
You want to spend more money.

00:56:28.930 --> 00:56:30.730
Obviously, you want to spend it wisely but
we push people.

00:56:30.730 --> 00:56:32.160
So in technology, I always do that.

00:56:32.160 --> 00:56:35.210
You can't ever say to me you didn't
do it because of the budget or

00:56:35.210 --> 00:56:35.920
you couldn't afford it.

00:56:35.920 --> 00:56:40.940
it is not allowed, you have to put in
your budget sort of a reflection of

00:56:40.940 --> 00:56:45.610
a 12 month of a plan, you have to put
in a plan everything you need to do.

00:56:45.610 --> 00:56:46.480
Going to sales force,

00:56:46.480 --> 00:56:49.250
going to companies going to technology,
do not be a baby about it,

00:56:49.250 --> 00:56:52.560
because people say, well we did not do it,
because we could not afford it, who said?

00:56:52.560 --> 00:56:54.050
We can afford anything we want.

00:56:54.050 --> 00:56:57.354
We spend $58 billion a year,
we make $35 billion pretax,

00:56:57.354 --> 00:57:01.283
if you guys put on the $2 billion to get
big data right, we're going to do it.

00:57:01.283 --> 00:57:05.566
So it's more about the getting executed
right than it is about the money.

00:57:05.566 --> 00:57:07.560
&gt;&gt; Everyone,
please join me in thanking Jamie Dimon.

00:57:07.560 --> 00:57:08.804
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much.

00:57:08.804 --> 00:57:13.672
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:57:13.672 --> 00:57:18.850
[MUSIC]

